
================================================================================
CORRECTION BANNER (klein-S336, filed after close-out on a late literature check)
================================================================================
 'SMALLEST OPEN DEGREE = 30' ABOVE IS WRONG.  It is the complement of {1,2,3,4}*p^k,
 i.e. the 2007 GvB-L-S-vdW result set only.  Later work (Draisma-de Jong 2011;
 Chellali 2012; Castryck-Laterveer-Ounaies, Math. Comp. 2014) covers 3p^k, 4p^k,
 5p^k, 6p^k, 7p^k with classified bad primes, plus degree 12, and d <= 19 is known.
 SMALLEST OPEN DEGREE PRE-2025 WAS d = 20.  Also: 3p^k and 4p^k carry prime
 exceptions my 'covered' predicate did not model.
 A full proof is CLAIMED: Soham Ghosh, arXiv:2501.09272 (v2 Mar 2026, 'Major
 revisions'), Koszul homology.  ACCEPTANCE UNVERIFIED; several earlier claimed CA
 proofs were never accepted.  Treat CA as open-with-a-claimed-proof-under-review.
 WHAT SURVIVES: the char-p map below.  The modern statement uses Hasse-Schmidt
 derivatives f_i = f^(i)/i!, which is exactly the fix for the vanishing-derivative
 artifacts found at p <= n.  The p > n witnesses SURVIVE unchanged, since i! is
 invertible for i < n < p, so HS and ordinary derivatives share roots: x^4 + x^2 =
 x^2(x-2)(x-3) over F_5 is still genuine, and 'mod-p certifies nothing for p > n'
 stands.  See HYP-8270-CORRECTION.
================================================================================
==============================================================================
(1) CASAS-ALVERO: which degrees are NOT covered by n = {1,2,3,4}*p^k ?
==============================================================================
 open degrees <= 200: [30, 35, 40, 42, 45, 55, 56, 60, 63, 65, 66, 70, 72, 77, 78, 80, 84, 85, 88, 90, 91, 95, 99, 102, 104, 105, 110, 112, 114, 115] ...
 SMALLEST OPEN DEGREE = 30   ( = 30 = 2*3*5 )
 every covered degree has at most 2 distinct primes: True
 every open degree has at least 3 distinct primes: False
 => THE CASAS-ALVERO BARRIER IS EXACTLY A THREE-DISTINCT-PRIMES PHENOMENON.
    n is covered iff n = c*p^k with c <= 4, and that forces omega(n) <= 2.

